   "text": "I read the drawing as a head-to-side-chain cyclic hexapeptide. The ring is closed through the Glu side chain and a 1,4-diaminobutane linker, and the N-terminus carries a 1-naphthylacetyl cap.\n\n**Connectivity, traced around the ring**\n- The cap is 1-naphthyl\u2013CH2\u2013C(=O)\u2013NH on the Glu \u03b1-nitrogen.\n- The Glu side chain runs CH2CH2C(=O)\u2013NH\u2013(CH2)4\u2013NH\u2013C(=O) and ends on the Trp \u03b1-carbon.\n- The Glu C=O links to the His NH, the His C=O to the 4-fluorophenylalanine NH, the Phe C=O to the Arg NH (guanidine side chain), and the Arg C=O to the Trp NH.\n- The Trp C=O closes the ring through the putrescine NH.\n- The indole is attached at C3, and the imidazole is attached through its 4-position.\n\n**Stereochemistry**\nFor Glu, the wedge on the side chain with N up and C=O lower-left gives S, which is the L configuration. For Arg, the hashed side chain also gives S (L). I took the His, Phe and Trp centres as L as well, without checking each wedge. After adjusting for the atom order in the SMILES, every ring \u03b1-carbon is written `[C@H]`.\n\nSMILES: O=C(Cc1cccc2ccccc12)N[C@H]1CCC(=O)NCCCCNC(=O)[C@H](Cc2c[nH]c3ccccc23)NC(=O)[C@H](CCCNC(=N)N)NC(=O)[C@H](Cc2ccc(F)cc2)NC(=O)[C@H](Cc2c[nH]cn2)NC1=O"
